                                    </div>

                                    <div class="faq-section" style="margin-top:40px;">
                                        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                                        <h3>What are the essential terms in a jewelry marketing agency contract?</h3>
                                        <p>10 essentials: (1) Specific quantified deliverables, (2) KPIs + measurement methodology, (3) SLA on issue resolution + escalation, (4) Ad spend billing transparency (no markup), (5) IP ownership + transfer clauses, (6) Team commitments + no-swap without approval, (7) Exit clauses + notice + milestone-based cancellation, (8) Data + platform access ownership (client-owned), (9) Confidentiality + NDA, (10) Payment terms + invoicing cadence.</p>
                                        <h3>Should we do a legal review of the MSA before signing?</h3>
                                        <p>Yes, always. Rs 15-40K corporate lawyer legal-review cost is well-invested. Rushed contracts + skipped legal review cost 3-10x during engagement or exit disputes. 2-3 negotiation rounds typical for well-drafted MSA. Total negotiation timeline 1-3 weeks.</p>
                                        <h3>What is the standard contract term for a jewelry marketing agency?</h3>
                                        <p>6-month MSA typical with month-to-month cancellation after month 3 if agreed milestones missed. 30-day notice standard. No early-termination penalty if agency missed milestones. Longer 12-24 month terms with lock-in are unusual and warrant scrutiny.</p>
                                        <h3>Who should own Meta Business Manager + Google Ads accounts?</h3>
                                        <p>Client-owned always. Agency has access during engagement. Contract should have explicit transfer clause: access revoked at exit + no data lock-in. Agency-owned platform accounts = data lock-in + creates artificial switching cost. Walk away if agency insists on owning.</p>
                                        <h3>What ad-spend billing arrangements should we insist on?</h3>
                                        <p>Transparent pass-through: Option A (client pays platforms directly + agency has management access) or Option B (agency pays + bills exactly what was spent with monthly attribution). No markup ever. Contract explicit + any exception requires written amendment.</p>
                                        <h3>What KPIs should be in the contract?</h3>
                                        <p>Metric definitions + measurement source-of-truth + reporting cadence + review milestones (30-day, 90-day, 6-month). Weekly ad spend + CPL + conversions + WhatsApp funnel + wishlist. Monthly LTV + CAC + payback + cohort + attribution. Quarterly brand-search lift + share-of-voice + editorial + celebrity metrics. Missing 90-day milestone should trigger cancellation right.</p>
                                        <h3>What are the red flags in agency contract proposals?</h3>
                                        <p>Auto-renewal without notice + bundled ad-spend + retainer + vague deliverables + agency-owned platform accounts + weak IP language + long notice without milestone escape + broad indemnification of agency + missing data + platform access transfer at exit. Any one is red flag; two or more = walk away.</p>
                                        <h3>How does ITD GrowthLabs structure jewelry client contracts?</h3>
                                        <p>6-month MSA + specific SOW per tier + monthly quantified deliverables + KPIs + milestone-based accountability + ad spend transparent pass-through + IP ownership to client + platform accounts client-owned + team + escalation path documented + 30-day notice + milestone-based cancellation after month 3. Standard corporate MSA + client-friendly SOW. See <a href="/industries/digital-marketing-for-jewelry-brands.php">jewelry vertical page</a>.</p>
